<Layout title="Editorial approach, sources & corrections | CleanCare" description="Learn who prepares CleanCare content, how sources and product claims are checked, how AI assistance is disclosed and how to report a correction."><section class="container page-intro"><p class="eyebrow">Why CleanCare exists</p><h1>Information first.<br/>Sources in view.</h1><div class="prose"><p>CleanCare is an educational site about fragrance exposure and health. It explains where exposure can happen, what research says about possible consequences, what remains uncertain, and practical ways to avoid or reduce exposure. It is not a product brand or store.</p><h2 id="editorial-method">Who prepares the content?</h2><p>CleanCare editorial is the publishing identity of this independent, founder-led education and advocacy project. It began with one family’s experience—not a medical qualification. <a href={url('about-us/')}>Read the founder’s story</a>.</p><p>Drafting, source organization, illustrations and website maintenance involve AI assistance. Linked sources allow readers to check important claims directly. The site is not independently medically reviewed; a doctor appearing in a linked video has not reviewed or endorsed CleanCare. We do not invent reviewer credentials or apply a medical-review stamp to AI-assisted writing.</p><h2>How articles are developed</h2><p>Community conversations can identify questions worth investigating, including topics discussed in the fragrance-sensitivity Facebook group. We do not present group posts as scientific proof or reproduce a member’s words without permission. Articles should trace important health claims to original research, public health agencies, or clearly identified expert presentations, and explain the limits of those sources. Source-checked does not mean independently tested or clinically validated.</p><p>Agency guidance and original research take priority for factual health claims. Expert opinions, documentaries, advocacy, commercial articles and personal accounts are labelled as different kinds of sources. Publication dates refer to CleanCare articles, not necessarily the age of their underlying sources. Update dates are added when the article is substantively revised—not automatically on every deployment.</p><h2>What the language means</h2><p>“Fragrance” covers many possible ingredients and mixtures. Health effects depend on the substance, exposure, and person. We describe documented concerns such as contact allergy and reported respiratory symptoms without claiming that every synthetic fragrance is toxic at normal use. A pleasant odor, a “natural” label, or no odor is not a reliable safety test.</p><h2 id="product-method">How product sources are checked</h2><p>Product recommendations are a secondary resource. We check exact versions against brand claims, current ingredient sources and country-specific listings—not whole brands. Profiles have direct non-affiliate links and limitations. Country choices appear only where catalogue items exist. Manufacturer claims are not independent testing or a promise that a product will suit everyone.</p><p>Fragrance-free is the default. Essential-oil alternatives are separately marked as scented and appear only through an explicit filter choice or direct profile link. A separately checked absence of essential oils is distinct from an unreviewed status. Formulas, packaging and availability can change: compare the current local label before choosing.</p><h2>Images and commercial interests</h2><p>Original AI-generated illustrations are labelled. Publisher and brand images retain source credits; a credit does not imply endorsement or independent testing. Reuse permissions should be confirmed, and uncertain assets can be replaced with original or appropriately licensed artwork. Expert brand founders’ commercial interests are disclosed. There are currently no paid placements or affiliate product links; any future relationship must be identified beside the recommendation.</p><h2>Individual health decisions</h2><p>Information on this site is educational. If a symptom is severe or persistent, seek advice from a qualified health professional. Botanical artwork is decorative and does not imply that houseplants clean indoor air.</p><h2 id="corrections">Contact and corrections</h2><p>Found an outdated product formula, inaccessible source or wording that needs correction? Email <a href="mailto:cleancare.admin@gmail.com?subject=CleanCare%20correction">cleancare.admin@gmail.com</a> with the page URL and the source that supports the correction. Please do not send medical records or sensitive personal health information.</p><p>Corrections should be checked against the source and reflected in the affected page; material article revisions should carry an update date. <a href={url('privacy/')}>Read the privacy explanation →</a></p></div></section></Layout>
